/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The flight instruments of an analog cockpit are the basic six, set out in the basic T: the attitude indicator at the top centre, the airspeed indicator to its left, the altimeter to its right, and the heading indicator below the attitude in
   - Examples: the Cessna 172 with its six-pack, the Boeing 727 and the early Boeing 747, where a flight engineer watched a separate panel of system dials.
   - An electronic flight instrument system (EFIS) has three parts: display units (the screens), display computers or symbol generators that turn sensor data into pictures, and control panels on which the crew selects modes and ranges.
   - A page is a screen layout.
   - A hybrid cockpit keeps some dials and adds screens; it was the normal step in the 1980s and is still a common upgrade.
   - A dial needs a sensor and a wire.
   - Software defects and bad data are the other risk: a screen can show a confident-looking wrong value.
*/
